{
  "raw": {
    "https://cuny.jobs/252F057C2AD04BDFA8490CE87D102AEC25": {
      "title": "Doctoral Lecturer in Spanish â€“ Specialist in Heritage Learners, Modern Languages and Literatures Department",
      "description": "Doctoral Lecturer in Spanish â€“ Specialist in Heritage Learners, Modern Languages and Literatures Department. Doctoral Lecturer in Spanish Specialist in Heritage Learners, Modern Languages and Literatures Department **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **ABOUT THE COLLEGE** John Jay College of Criminal Justice is a senior college of the City University of New York (CUNY) and an internationally recognized leader in educating for justice. Led by President Karol V. Mason, John Jay is a federally designated Hispanic-serving institution, it is ranked third in the nation in Black student success, and it is a top ten institution for promoting student social mobility. John Jay is proud to serve a diverse and dynamic student body of 15,000 students that includes nearly fifty percent students who are first in their family to attend college as well as students who are immigrants, from low-income families, or from other historically underrepresented groups in higher education. The College participates in the doctoral programs of the Graduate Center of CUNY and offers bachelors and masters degrees both in traditional criminal justice-related fields of study as well as in a robust portfolio of liberal arts and sciences programs that highlight themes of justice across the arts, sciences, humanities, and social sciences. The College seeks staff and faculty members who thrive in multicultural academic environments and are committed to access and excellence in higher education. **POSITION OVERVIEW** The Department of Modern Languages and Literatures at John Jay College-CUNY seeks an instructor at the rank of Doctoral Lecturer with experience teaching Spanish for heritage learners. The position of Doctoral Lecturer is tenure-bearing through a Certificate of Continuous Employment (CCE) following the sixth annual reappointment. Lecturers become eligible for promotion to the rank of Senior Lecturer after eight years of full-time continuous service. The appointed faculty member will perform teaching and related faculty functions in areas of expertise, share responsibility for departmental and College committee work and other duties as assigned by the chair. Teaching duties include a 4/4 load (eight undergraduate courses per year). Service duties include actively recruiting, advising and mentoring Spanish majors, minors, and Certificate students, among others. Responsibilities will include working collaboratively with colleagues, the department, and the College. The hiring committee is especially interested in applicants who contribute to the goals of the college through their leadership, community service, and/or lived experiences. The successful candidate should be enthusiastic and well-prepared to work with students from a wide range of backgrounds and demonstrate a commitment to fostering an inclusive and supportive learning environment. As demonstrated in John Jay Colleges Seven Principles for a Culturally Responsive, Inclusive, and Anti-Racist Curriculum (https://www.jjay.cuny.edu/john-jays-seven-principles), the College seeks a faculty member who thrives in a multicultural, collaborative academic environment and is committed to both access and excellence in higher education. The Department of Modern Languages and Literatures offers a BA in Spanish with concentrations in Spanish<>English Legal Translation/Interpretation, and Spanish and Latin American Literatures and Cultures; a Spanish minor; and three Certificates in Legal Translation and/or Interpretation. It also offers L2 courses in American Sign Language, Arabic, Chinese, French, German, Italian, Japanese and Portuguese. For Spanish, both L2 and Heritage tracks are offered. **QUALIFICATIONS** By the date of appointment, applicants must possess a PhD degree in Spanish linguistics with a focus in Heritage learners or in a related discipline from a regionally accredited U.S. institution or an equivalent non-U.S. institution. Applicants must also have the ability to effectively teach Spanish for heritage learners at all levels of undergraduate instruction. The successful candidate may also be expected to teach Spanish (L2) language courses at all levels of undergraduate instruction. Candidates are expected to bring enthusiasm and demonstrated commitment to both teaching and service. All CUNY employees must reside within a commutable distance to their campus. **OTHER QUALIFICATIONS / PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS** Preference will be given to candidates who meet the following qualifications: three years of experience teaching Spanish for Heritage learners; experience using innovative teaching methods to effectively engage a diverse student body; experience in curriculum design; demonstrated ability to teach effectively in diverse modalities; experience and interest in developing internships, community outreach, and service learning. Lecturers at CUNY do not have an expectation of scholarly productivity, but we welcome and will support applicants with active research agendas. Ph.D. candidates (including ABDs) are encouraged to apply. **COMPENSATION** $62,241 - $97,487 ( _As of 9/1/2026 $64,419- $100,899_ ). Salary commensurate with education and credentials. CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** If you are viewing the job posting on any website other than CUNYFirst, please follow the instructions below: - Go to www.cuny.edu and click on Employment (https://www.cuny.edu/employment/) - Click Search job postings on the left menu. Then click All postings - Search by job Opening ID number 32644 - Click on the Apply Now button and follow the instructions Once you have registered or logged in with your username and password, upload the following materials as a single PDF document: (1) cover letter, (2) curriculum vitae, (3) a teaching portfolio, including teaching philosophy, teaching evaluations, sample syllabi and materials. Please have three professional letters of recommendation sent to: Erica L. Wise Department of Modern Languages and Literatures 524 West 59th Street, 7.65.01 212-237-8577 ewise@jjay.cuny.edu **Official transcripts are not required at this time but will be required upon employment.** **CLOSING DATE** Posting closes November 1. Review of applications begins November 2. **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32644 Location: John Jay College Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/B9A6A1A6859D415EB725608E20421B0225": {
      "title": "Lecturer of Spanish â€“ Specialist in Translation and Interpreting, Modern Languages and Literatures Department",
      "description": "Lecturer of Spanish â€“ Specialist in Translation and Interpreting, Modern Languages and Literatures Department. Lecturer of Spanish Specialist in Translation and Interpreting, Modern Languages and Literatures Department **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **ABOUT THE COLLEGE** John Jay College of Criminal Justice is a senior college of the City University of New York (CUNY) and an internationally recognized leader in educating for justice. Led by President Karol V. Mason, John Jay is a federally designated Hispanic-serving institution, it is ranked third in the nation in Black student success, and it is a top ten institution for promoting student social mobility. John Jay is proud to serve a diverse and dynamic student body of 15,000 students that includes nearly fifty percent students who are first in their family to attend college as well as students who are immigrants, from low-income families, or from other historically underrepresented groups in higher education. The College participates in the doctoral programs of the Graduate Center of CUNY and offers bachelors and masters degrees both in traditional criminal justice-related fields of study as well as in a robust portfolio of liberal arts and sciences programs that highlight themes of justice across the arts, sciences, humanities, and social sciences. The College seeks staff and faculty members who thrive in multicultural academic environments and are committed to access and excellence in higher education. **POSITION OVERVIEW** The Department of Modern Languages and Literatures at John Jay College-CUNY seeks applications from individuals with experience teaching Spanish<>English Translation & Interpreting for a full-time Lecturer position to begin Fall 2027. The position of Lecturer is tenure-bearing through a Certificate of Continuous Employment (CCE) following the sixth annual reappointment. Lecturers become eligible for promotion to the rank of Senior Lecturer after eight years of full-time continuous service, and for the rank of Doctoral Lecturer upon completion of a Ph.D. The appointed faculty member will perform teaching and related faculty functions in areas of expertise, share responsibility for departmental and College committee work and other duties as assigned by the chair. Teaching duties include a 4/4 load (eight undergraduate courses per year). Service duties include actively recruiting, advising and mentoring Spanish majors, minors, and Certificate students, among others. Responsibilities will include working collaboratively with colleagues, the department, and the College. The hiring committee is especially interested in applicants who contribute to the goals of the college through their leadership, community service, and/or lived experiences. The successful candidate should be enthusiastic and well-prepared to work with students from a wide range of backgrounds and demonstrate a commitment to fostering an inclusive and supportive learning environment. As demonstrated in John Jay Colleges Seven Principles for a Culturally Responsive, Inclusive, and Anti-Racist Curriculum (https://www.jjay.cuny.edu/john-jays-seven-principles), the College seeks a faculty member who thrives in a multicultural, collaborative academic environment and is committed to both access and excellence in higher education. The Department of Modern Languages and Literatures offers a BA in Spanish with concentrations in Spanish<>English Legal Translation/Interpretation, and Spanish and Latin American Literatures and Cultures; a Spanish minor; and three Certificates in Legal Translation and/or Interpretation. It also offers L2 courses in American Sign Language, Arabic, Chinese, French, German, Italian, Japanese and Portuguese. For Spanish, both L2 and Heritage tracks are offered **QUALIFICATIONS** By the date of appointment, applicants must possess a Master's degree from a regionally accredited U.S. institution or an equivalent non-U.S. institution in one of the following: (1) Translation and/or Interpreting Studies; (2) a related discipline with a demonstrable academic focus in translation and/or interpreting; or (3) another discipline together with demonstrable professional experience in translation and/or interpreting. Applicants must also have the ability to effectively teach in at least two of the following areas at all levels of undergraduate instruction: (1) Spanish translation (general and/or legal); (2) Spanish interpreting (general and/or legal); and (3) Spanish for heritage learners. The successful candidate may also be expected to teach Spanish (L2) language courses at all levels of undergraduate instruction. Candidates are expected to bring enthusiasm and demonstrated commitment to both teaching and service. All CUNY employees must reside within a commutable distance to their campus. **OTHER QUALIFICATIONS / PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS** Preference will be given to candidates who meet the following qualifications: Experience teaching specialized Spanish<>English translation and interpreting (legal, medical, business); experience using innovative teaching methods and/or curricular design to effectively engage a diverse student body; experience and/or interest in developing internships, community outreach and service learning; and ability to teach effectively in different modalities. Lecturers at CUNY do not have an expectation of scholarly productivity, but we welcome and will support applicants with active research agendas. Ph.D. candidates (including ABDs) are encouraged to apply. **COMPENSATION** $57,039 - $91,753 ( _As of 9/1/2026 $59,035- $94,965_ ). Salary commensurate with education and credentials. CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** If you are viewing the job posting on any website other than CUNYFirst, please follow the instructions below: - Go to www.cuny.edu and click on Employment (https://www.cuny.edu/employment/) - Click Search job postings on the left menu. Then click All postings - Search by job Opening ID number 32645 - Click on the Apply Now button and follow the instructions Once you have registered or logged in with your username and password, upload the following materials as a single PDF document: (1) cover letter, (2) curriculum vitae, (3) a teaching portfolio, including teaching philosophy, teaching evaluations, sample syllabi and materials. Please have three professional letters of recommendation sent to: Erica L. Wise Department of Modern Languages and Literatures 524 West 59th Street, 7.65.01 212-237-8577 ewise@jjay.cuny.edu Official transcripts are not required at this time but will be required upon employment. **CLOSING DATE** Posting closes October 26. Review of applications begins November 2. **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32645 Location: John Jay College Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/815A9FD1998A490883C6967F340A793D25": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor â€“ Department of Economics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor â€“ Department of Economics. Assistant Professor Department of Economics **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **ABOUT THE COLLEGE** John Jay College of Criminal Justice is a senior college of the City University of New York (CUNY) and an internationally recognized leader in educating for justice. Led by President Karol V. Mason, John Jay is a federally designated Hispanic-serving institution, it is ranked third in the nation in Black student success, and it is a top ten institution for promoting student social mobility. John Jay is proud to serve a diverse and dynamic student body of 15,000 students that includes nearly fifty percent students who are first in their family to attend college as well as students who are immigrants, from low-income families, or from other historically underrepresented groups in higher education. The College participates in the doctoral programs of the Graduate Center of CUNY, and offers bachelors and masters degrees both in traditional criminal justice-related fields of study as well as in a robust portfolio of liberal arts and sciences programs that highlight themes of justice across the arts, sciences, humanities, and social sciences. The College seeks staff and faculty members who thrive in multicultural academic environments and are committed to access and excellence in higher education. **POSITION OVERVIEW** The Economics Department of John Jay College invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in any field at the Assistant Professor level to begin Fall 2027. The department is dedicated to pluralism and diversity; applicants with a background in heterodox economics are strongly encouraged to apply. The successful applicant will teach at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, mentor undergraduate and graduate students, develop curricula, serve as course coordinator, and interact effectively with colleagues to support and enhance department culture. The hiring committee is especially interested in applicants who contribute to the goals of the college through their leadership, community service, research, and/or lived experiences. Candidates are expected to bring enthusiasm and demonstrated commitment to teaching and to develop and maintain an active research and publication agenda. Individuals from demographic groups underrepresented in the economics profession are particularly encouraged to apply. The successful candidate should be enthusiastic and well-prepared to work with students from a wide range of backgrounds and demonstrate a commitment to fostering an inclusive and supportive learning environment. Consistent with John Jay Colleges Seven Principles for a Culturally Responsive, Inclusive, and Anti-Racist Curriculum ( https://www.jjay.cuny.edu/john-jays-seven-principles ), the College seeks faculty who thrive in a multicultural, collaborative academic environment and are committed to both access and excellence in higher education. **QUALIFICATIONS** Ph.D. degree in Economics or equivalent. Also required are the ability to teach successfully, the potential for scholarly productivity, willingness to participate in course and program development, and the ability to work with others for the good of institution. Candidates are expected to bring enthusiasm and a commitment to teaching, service and scholarship. **COMPENSATION** Salary commensurate with academic accomplishments and experience within the range of $73,294 - $102,460. CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** 1) If viewing the job posting on John Jay College website or in CUNYFirst, please select the \"Apply Now\" button. If viewing the job posting on any other website, please follow the instructions below: -Go to www.cuny.edu/employment -Click \"Search job postings. -Click the link for Faculty and browse to job Opening ID number 32642 -Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Once registered or logged in, candidates should submit the following: an application letter to include how they would contribute to the goals of the College, C.V., research statement, job market paper, and a statement of teaching philosophy, including a discussion of how to create an equitable and accessible learning environment for our students. All items to be uploaded must be combined in a single document preferably in PDF format. 2) In addition, please have three reference letters sent to EconomicsPosition@jjay.cuny.edu. Here candidates can also submit any supplemental materials, not submitted over CUNYFirst. For additional information email the chair of the Economics Department: J.W. Mason, jomason@jjay.cuny.edu **CLOSING DATE** Review of applications to begin on October 15, 2026. Position closes on November 1, 2026. **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32642 Location: John Jay College Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/2725D9DA80A84B4FA750641A8A9D45CE25": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Molecular Biology/Forensic Biology, Department of Sciences",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Molecular Biology/Forensic Biology, Department of Sciences. Assistant Professor - Molecular Biology/Forensic Biology, Department of Sciences **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **ABOUT THE COLLEGE** John Jay College of Criminal Justice is a senior college of the City University of New York (CUNY) and an internationally recognized leader in educating for justice. Led by President Karol V. Mason, John Jay is a federally designated Hispanic-serving institution, ranked third in the nation in Black student success, and a top-ten institution for promoting student social mobility. John Jay is proud to serve a diverse and dynamic student body of 15,000 students, including nearly 50% who are first in their family to attend college, as well as students who are immigrants, from low-income families, or from other historically underrepresented groups in higher education. The College participates in the doctoral programs of the Graduate Center of CUNY and offers bachelors and masters degrees both in traditional criminal justice-related fields of study as well as in a robust portfolio of liberal arts and sciences programs that highlight themes of justice across the arts, sciences, humanities, and social sciences. **POSITION OVERVIEW** The Department of Sciences at John Jay College of Criminal Justice invites applications for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor in Molecular Biology, with expertise relevant to forensic biology, to begin Fall 2027. We seek an enthusiastic and collaborative scholar-educator who will contribute to our nationally recognized forensic science programs and help prepare the next generation of forensic scientists. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate and graduate courses in molecular biology, genetics, forensic biology, and related areas within the Forensic Science Program, while developing an active research agenda that complements the mission of the Department and the College. The ideal candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in teaching, mentoring, and research. We are especially interested in candidates with the potential to engage students in meaningful research experiences and to develop externally funded projects in molecular biology, forensic biology, forensic genetics, or closely related areas. Candidates should have a record of scholarly productivity or demonstrate strong potential for publishing in peer-reviewed scientific journals. Responsibilities include teaching courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels, mentoring students in research, contributing to curriculum development in forensic biology, participating in departmental and college service, and developing a sustainable research program. The candidate should demonstrate a commitment to working collaboratively with faculty, students, and external partners, be enthusiastic and well-prepared to work with students from a wide range of backgrounds, and demonstrate a commitment to fostering an inclusive and supportive learning environment. As demonstrated in John Jay Colleges Seven Principles for a Culturally Responsive, Inclusive, and Anti-Racist Curriculum (https://www.jjay.cuny.edu/john-jays-seven-principles), the College seeks a faculty member who thrives in a multicultural, collaborative academic environment and is committed to both access and excellence in higher education. John Jay College offers a unique opportunity to work in a dynamic academic environment dedicated to justice, science, and public service. The Department of Sciences is home to rigorous forensic science programs that combine strong scientific training with real-world applications, preparing students for careers in forensic laboratories, research institutions, graduate and professional programs, and public service. **QUALIFICATIONS** A Ph.D. in Molecular Biology, Forensic Biology, Genetics, Biochemistry, Cell Biology, or a closely related field is required. Professional experience in forensic science, forensic biology, DNA analysis, forensic genetics, or related laboratory practice is strongly preferred. Experience teaching or mentoring undergraduate or graduate students, a strong publication record or evidence of scholarly potential, and the ability to develop an externally funded research program are strongly valued. **COMPENSATION** $59,256 - $114,091. Salary commensurate with education and credentials CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** If you are viewing the job posting on any website other than CUNYFirst, please follow the instructions below: 1. Navigate to www.cuny.edu 2. Select Employment 3. Select Search Job Postings 4. Create an Account 5. After creating an account, return to the Job Search page and select Apply Now Once you have registered or logged in with your username and password, upload the following documents as a single PDF. Only applications submitted through CUNYFirst are eligible for consideration. Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Statement of scholarly interests Statement of teaching philosophy Statement of how you would contribute to the Colleges goals for access and academic excellence Proof of degree Candidates should have three letters of recommendation emailed to the following address: SCIENCE@JJAY.CUNY.EDU with the subject title as Job ID 32641 Recommendation letter. **CLOSING DATE** The review of resumes will begin on October 2, 2026 The posting closes on November 1, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32641 Location: John Jay College Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/0BBBD02883514BBF818ECA4829F7EB2225": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor â€“ 2 positions - Department of Law, Police Science and CJA",
      "description": "Assistant Professor â€“ 2 positions - Department of Law, Police Science and CJA. Assistant Professor 2 positions - Department of Law, Police Science and CJA **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **ABOUT THE COLLEGE** John Jay College of Criminal Justice is a senior college of the City University of New York (CUNY) and an internationally recognized leader in educating for justice. Led by President Karol V. Mason, John Jay is a federally designated Hispanic-serving institution, it is ranked third in the nation in Black student success, and it is a top ten institution for promoting student social mobility. John Jay is proud to serve a diverse and dynamic student body of 15,000 students that includes nearly fifty percent students who are first in their family to attend college as well as students who are immigrants, from low-income families, or from other historically underrepresented groups in higher education. The College participates in the doctoral programs of the Graduate Center of CUNY, and offers bachelors and masters degrees both in traditional criminal justice-related fields of study as well as in a robust portfolio of liberal arts and sciences programs that highlight themes of justice across the arts, sciences, humanities, and social sciences. The College seeks staff and faculty members who thrive in multicultural academic environments and are committed to access and excellence in higher education. **POSITION OVERVIEW** The Department of Law, Police Science, and Criminal Justice Administration (LPS) at John Jay College of Criminal Justice invites applications for two tenure-track positions at the rank of Assistant Professor to begin Fall 2027. The successful candidates will teach security related courses in our undergraduate Security major and minors in Homeland Security and Cyber Crime, from entry level up to capstone seminars. The hiring committee is especially interested in applicants who contribute to the goals of the college through their teaching, leadership, community service, research, and/or lived experiences. The prospective candidates should have expertise and publications with a focus on various aspects of private security. Course load is six (6) classes per year. Significant course release is available for newly appointed faculty. Teaching, departmental and college service, and an active research and publishing agenda are expected. The Department of Law, Police Science, and Criminal Justice Administration is a Flagship Department of John Jay College of Criminal Justice. We are an interdisciplinary department whose faculty specialize in criminal justice broadly defined. This includes the fields of criminal justice, international criminal justice, policing, law, courts, and corrections. LPS is home to the Criminal Justice Bachelor of Science (CJBS) major which enrolls more than 3,000 undergraduate students, the Police Science Major, Security Management Major and Masters, and on a rotating basis, the International Criminal Justice Major. The successful candidate should be enthusiastic and well-prepared to work with students from a wide range of backgrounds and demonstrate a commitment to fostering an inclusive and supportive learning environment. As demonstrated in John Jay Colleges Seven Principles for a Culturally Responsive, Inclusive, and Anti-Racist Curriculum -- https://www.jjay.cuny.edu/john-jays-seven-principles -- the College seeks a faculty member who thrives in a multicultural, collaborative academic environment and is committed to both access and excellence in higher education. **QUALIFICATIONS** **Minimum Qualifications** Candidates must possess: 1. PhD degree in Security Management, Homeland Security, Cybersecurity, Information Security, Criminal Justice, or a closely related field. 2. Demonstrated professional experience in one or more areas of security management, cybersecurity, homeland security, risk management, intelligence analysis, emergency management, corporate security, critical infrastructure protection, or related fields. 3. Evidence of teaching experience, training delivery, student mentorship, or professional instructional experience in higher education, government, military, law enforcement, or private-sector settings. 4. Ability to teach undergraduate courses in security management and contribute to curriculum development in emerging areas of security and technology. 5. Research and publication(s) on related security topics in peer-reviewed outlets, such as in the areas of Info/Cyber Security and Security and Technology **Preferred Qualifications** Strong candidates will possess one or more of the following: 1. Professional experience in cybersecurity, information assurance, intelligence analysis, critical infrastructure protection, corporate security, emergency management, risk assessment, or security operations. 2. Demonstrated ability to teach and/or develop courses in areas such as: Cybersecurity Fundamentals, Information Systems for Management, Data Analytics and Data Visualization; Cloud Computing and Information Security, Risk Management, Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery, Intelligence and Threat Analysis, Security Management Ethics, Critical Infrastructure Protection, Artificial Intelligence, Security Policies, Research Methods, Machine Learning; Security Risk and Technology; Risk and Vulnerability Analysis. 3. Industry certifications, such as: CISSP, CISM, Security+, Certified Ethical Hacker (CEH), Certified Information Systems Auditor (CISA), Certified Protection Professional (CPP), Emergency Management certifications (FEMA, IAEM, etc.) 4. Experience working collaboratively in academic environments, including program assessment, curriculum revision, student advising, online instruction, accreditation activities, or service to the institution. The Department is currently engaged in exciting efforts to modernize and expand its Security Management curriculum. We are particularly interested in candidates whose professional expertise can help prepare students for careers in cybersecurity, intelligence and threat analysis, critical infrastructure protection, corporate security, risk management, data analytics, and emerging technologies. Practitioner-scholars who can bridge theory and practice and bring real-world experience into the classroom are especially encouraged to apply. **COMPENSATION** $59,526 - $102,460. Salary commensurate with education and credentials. CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** If you are viewing the job posting on any website other than CUNYfirst, please follow the instructions below: - Navigate to www.cuny.edu - Select Employment - Select Search Job Postings -Click the link for Faculty and browse to job Opening ID number 32781 -Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions Once registered or logged in, candidates should submit the following: an application letter with a statement of teaching interests, C.V., names of three references along with contact information, and proof of degree or progress toward the degree. All items to be uploaded must be combined in a single document preferably in PDF format. **CLOSING DATE** Review of resumes to begin October 30, 2026. Posting closes on November 30, 2026. **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32781 Location: John Jay College Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/720ECCCF8750458495512902B77D4AB925": {
      "title": "Lecturer â€“ 2 positions, Department of Law, Police Science and CJA",
      "description": "Lecturer â€“ 2 positions, Department of Law, Police Science and CJA. Lecturer 2 positions, Department of Law, Police Science and CJA **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **ABOUT THE COLLEGE** John Jay College of Criminal Justice is a senior college of the City University of New York (CUNY) and an internationally recognized leader in educating for justice. Led by President Karol V. Mason, John Jay is a federally designated Hispanic-serving institution, it is ranked third in the nation in Black student success, and it is a top ten institution for promoting student social mobility. John Jay is proud to serve a diverse and dynamic student body of 15,000 students that includes nearly fifty percent students who are first in their family to attend college as well as students who are immigrants, from low-income families, or from other historically underrepresented groups in higher education. The College participates in the doctoral programs of the Graduate Center of CUNY, and offers bachelors and masters degrees both in traditional criminal justice-related fields of study as well as in a robust portfolio of liberal arts and sciences programs that highlight themes of justice across the arts, sciences, humanities, and social sciences. The College seeks staff and faculty members who thrive in multicultural academic environments and are committed to access and excellence in higher education. **POSITION OVERVIEW** The Department of Law, Police Science, and Criminal Justice Administration (LPS) at John Jay College of Criminal Justice invites applications for two full-time lecturer positions to begin Fall 2027. The successful candidates will teach security related courses in our undergraduate Security major and minors in Homeland Security and Cyber Crime, from entry level up to capstone seminars. This includes courses such as Management and Leadership; Security and the Law; Business Planning and Budgeting, and Project Management courses. Depending on qualifications, there may be opportunity to teach online graduate level courses in the Security Management Masters. Candidates must be willing to serve as the coordinator of the Security related major and minors which includes assessment and curricular development for the major and minors. Candidates are expected to have successful college-level teaching experience, as well as positive teaching evaluations. Candidates are expected to have prior expertise as practitioners and in teaching courses in the area of private security, particularly information/cyber security. The hiring committee is especially interested in applicants who contribute to the goals of the college through their leadership, community service, research, and/or lived experiences. The position of lecturer is tenure-bearing through what is called a certificate of continuous employment (CCE) at the sixth annual appointment. This position of lecturer is a teaching position with a course load of eight classes per year in addition to major expectations of departmental service. There is no expectation of research or publication. The Department of Law, Police Science, and Criminal Justice Administration is a Flagship Department of John Jay College of Criminal Justice. We are an interdisciplinary department whose faculty specialize in criminal justice broadly defined. This includes the fields of criminal justice, international criminal justice, policing, law, courts, and corrections. LPS is home to the Criminal Justice Bachelor of Science (CJBS) major which enrolls more than 3,000 undergraduate students, the Police Science Major, Security Management Major and Masters, and on a rotating basis, the International Criminal Justice Major. The successful candidate should be enthusiastic and well-prepared to work with students from a wide range of backgrounds and demonstrate a commitment to fostering an inclusive and supportive learning environment. As demonstrated in John Jay Colleges Seven Principles for a Culturally Responsive, Inclusive, and Anti-Racist Curriculum -- https://www.jjay.cuny.edu/john-jays-seven-principles -- the College seeks a faculty member who thrives in a multicultural, collaborative academic environment and is committed to both access and excellence in higher education. **QUALIFICATIONS** **Minimum Qualifications** Candidates must possess: 1. Master's degree in Security Management, Homeland Security, Cybersecurity, Information Security, Emergency Management, Criminal Justice, Public Administration, or a closely related field. 2. Demonstrated professional experience in one or more areas of security management, cybersecurity, homeland security, risk management, intelligence analysis, emergency management, corporate security, critical infrastructure protection, or related fields. 3. Evidence of teaching experience, training delivery, student mentorship, or professional instructional experience in higher education, government, military, law enforcement, or private-sector settings. 4. Ability to teach undergraduate courses in security management and contribute to curriculum development in emerging areas of security and technology. **Preferred Qualifications** Strong candidates will possess one or more of the following: 1. Professional experience in cybersecurity, information assurance, intelligence analysis, critical infrastructure protection, corporate security, emergency management, risk assessment, or security operations. 2. Demonstrated ability to teach and/or develop courses in areas such as: Cybersecurity Fundamentals, Information Systems for Management, Data Analytics and Data Visualization; Cloud Computing and Information Security, Risk Management, Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery, Intelligence and Threat Analysis, Security Management Ethics, Critical Infrastructure Protection, Artificial Intelligence, Security Applications. 3. Industry certifications, such as: CISSP, CISM, Security+, Certified Ethical Hacker (CEH), Certified Information Systems Auditor (CISA), Certified Protection Professional (CPP), Emergency Management certifications (FEMA, IAEM, etc.) 4. Experience working collaboratively in academic environments, including program assessment, curriculum revision, student advising, online instruction, accreditation activities, or service to the institution. The Department is currently engaged in exciting efforts to modernize and expand its Security Management curriculum. We are particularly interested in candidates whose professional expertise can help prepare students for careers in cybersecurity, intelligence and threat analysis, critical infrastructure protection, corporate security, risk management, data analytics, and emerging technologies. Practitioner-scholars who can bridge theory and practice and bring real-world experience into the classroom are encouraged to apply. **COMPENSATION** $59,035 - $94,965. Salary commensurate with education and credentials. CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** If you are viewing the job posting on any website other than CUNYfirst, please follow the instructions below: - Navigate to www.cuny.edu - Select Employment - Select Search Job Postings -Click the link for Faculty and browse to job Opening ID number 32782 -Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions Once registered or logged in, candidates should submit the following: an application letter with a statement of teaching interests, C.V., names of three references along with contact information, and proof of degree or progress toward the degree. All items to be uploaded must be combined in a single document preferably in PDF format. **CLOSING DATE** Review of resumes to begin October 30, 2026. Posting closes on November 30, 2026. **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32782 Location: John Jay College Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/D7A506B4A8E44B7C89027B71969620A925": {
      "title": "Doctoral Lecturer - Anatomy and Physiology, Department of Sciences",
      "description": "Doctoral Lecturer - Anatomy and Physiology, Department of Sciences. Doctoral Lecturer - Anatomy and Physiology, Department of Sciences **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **ABOUT THE COLLEGE** John Jay College of Criminal Justice is a senior college of the City University of New York (CUNY) and an internationally recognized leader in educating for justice. Led by President Karol V. Mason, John Jay is a federally designated Hispanic-serving institution, ranked third in the nation in Black student success, and a top-ten institution for promoting student social mobility. John Jay is proud to serve a diverse and dynamic student body of 15,000 students, including nearly 50% who are first in their family to attend college, as well as students who are immigrants, from low-income families, or from other historically underrepresented groups in higher education. The College participates in the doctoral programs of the Graduate Center of CUNY and offers bachelors and masters degrees both in traditional criminal justice-related fields of study as well as in a robust portfolio of liberal arts and sciences programs that highlight themes of justice across the arts, sciences, humanities, and social sciences. **POSITION OVERVIEW** The Department of Sciences at John Jay College of Criminal Justice invites applications for a full-time Doctoral Lecturer position beginning in Fall 2027. The successful candidate will teach biology courses within our undergraduate majors and minors, from entry-level courses to capstone seminars, with an emphasis on human anatomy and physiology. Candidates must have a strong track record in science education with experience teaching human anatomy and physiology lectures and labs. Candidates must also be willing to serve as academic advisors to students in the science majors and minors. Three years of successful college-level teaching experience and positive teaching evaluations are required. The hiring committee is especially interested in applicants who contribute to the college's goals through their leadership, community service, and/or lived experiences. The position of lecturer is tenure-bearing through what is called a certificate of continuous employment (CCE) after the fourth annual reappointment. This position of lecturer is a teaching position with a course load of eight classes per year in addition to significant contribution of service to the department. There is no expectation of research or publication. The Department of Sciences at John Jay College of Criminal Justice is a vibrant community ranked among the oldest and most prestigious in the country in the field of forensic science. Faculties are internationally recognized for their diverse expertise in chemistry, cellular and molecular biology, toxicology, entomology, pathology, and criminalistics. The Department offers a BS in Forensic Science (which includes the three tracks of Criminalistics, Molecular Biology and Toxicology), a BS in Toxicology and a BS in Cellular & Molecular Biology. A Master of Science in Forensic Science is also offered in the three tracks previously listed. The Department also offers an accelerated BS/MS program. Candidates are expected to bring enthusiasm and a demonstrated commitment to teaching. The successful candidate should be eager and well-prepared to work with students from a wide range of backgrounds and demonstrate a commitment to fostering an inclusive and supportive learning environment. Consistent with John Jay Colleges Seven Principles for a Culturally Responsive, Inclusive, and Anti-Racist Curriculum (https://www.jjay.cuny.edu/john-jays-seven-principles), the College seeks a faculty member who thrives in a multicultural, collaborative academic environment and is committed to both access and excellence in higher education. **QUALIFICATIONS** + Candidates must have a strong background in both science education and biological sciences, and specifically anatomy and physiology. Candidates with a doctoral degree are strongly preferred. This could be a PhD in a biomedical science with strong teaching credentials, experience, and/or a publication record in the science education literature, or it could be a PhD or EdD in science education coupled with postgraduate education and/or experience working or training in biomedical science. Candidates with postdoctoral training in either science education or a relevant biomedical science will receive increased consideration. Candidates who are ABD may apply, but must have the degree conferred prior to start date. + Three years of undergraduate teaching experience in human anatomy and physiology (lectures and labs) preferred. + Candidate should demonstrate a proven track record of teaching success by integrating emerging digital technologies and pedagogical techniques into biology education, alongside expertise in course development and student learning assessment. **COMPENSATION** $64,419 - $111,455. Salary commensurate with education and credentials. CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** If you are viewing the job posting on any website other than CUNYFirst, please follow the instructions below: 1. Navigate to www.cuny.edu 2. Select Employment 3. Select Search Job Postings 4. Create an Account 5. After creating an account, return to the Job Search page and select Apply Now Once you have registered or logged in, candidates should submit the following: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Statement of teaching philosophy A proof of degree or progress toward the degree Names and Contacts of three professional references All items to be uploaded must be combined in a single document preferably in PDF format. Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst or the John Jay College Website are eligible for consideration. **CLOSING DATE** Review of resumes to begin on December 1, 2026. Posting will close on November 30, 2026. **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32833 Location: John Jay College Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/EB50AB0A1A494B4180109849674CC50925": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Department of Mathematics and Computer Science",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Department of Mathematics and Computer Science. Assistant Professor - Department of Mathematics and Computer Science **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **ABOUT THE COLLEGE** John Jay College of Criminal Justice is a senior college of the City University of New York (CUNY) and an internationally recognized leader in educating for justice. Led by President Karol V. Mason, John Jay is a federally designated Hispanic-serving institution, it is ranked third in the nation in Black student success, and it is a top ten institution for promoting student social mobility. John Jay is proud to serve a diverse and dynamic student body of 15,000 students that includes nearly fifty percent students who are first in their family to attend college as well as students who are immigrants, from low-income families, or from other historically underrepresented groups in higher education. The College participates in the doctoral programs of the Graduate Center of CUNY, and offers bachelors and masters degrees both in traditional criminal justice-related fields of study as well as in a robust portfolio of liberal arts and sciences programs that highlight themes of justice across the arts, sciences, humanities, and social sciences. The College seeks staff and faculty members who thrive in multicultural academic environments and are committed to access and excellence in higher education. **POSITION OVERVIEW** The Mathematics & Computer Science Department of John Jay College of Criminal Justice (CUNY) invites applications for a tenure track faculty member at the rank of assistant professor to begin Fall semester 2027. The department currently employs 17 tenured/tenure-track and 21 lecturer faculty who together support a thriving undergraduate Computer Science major, an Applied Mathematics major as well as a graduate program in Digital Forensics and Cybersecurity. This tenure-track position seeks to expand our expertise at the intersection of Cybersecurity and AI with special emphasis on Cloud Security, Risk Management, Digital Forensics and Cyber Threat Intelligence. The ideal candidate will contribute to both the undergraduate and graduate programs and strengthen our research and education offerings in cloud computing, cloud security, digital forensics and cybersecurity. The position of Assistant Professor is tenure-bearing after the seventh annual reappointment. This position comes with a course load of six courses (18 credits) per year. These courses may be at both graduate and undergraduate levels. In addition, the position has an expectation of scholarship through research as well as expectation of departmental service through involvement in committees. New faculty members receive contractual course release time to engage in research and scholarship both pre-tenure (18 hours) and post-tenure (6 hours). Candidates are expected to bring enthusiasm and demonstrated commitment to teaching and to develop and maintain an active research and publication agenda. The successful candidate must be eager and well-prepared to work with students from a wide range of backgrounds and demonstrate a commitment to fostering an inclusive and supportive learning environment. Consistent with John Jay Colleges Seven Principles for a Culturally Responsive, Inclusive, and Anti-Racist Curriculum -- https://www.jjay.cuny.edu/john-jays-seven-principles -- the College seeks a faculty member who thrives in a multicultural, collaborative academic environment and is committed to both access and excellence in higher education. **QUALIFICATIONS** The ideal candidate will be enthusiastic about teaching wide array of cybersecurity, cloud, incident response, risk assessment, offensive/defensive security and digital forensics topics. Competency in the systems area of computer science such as virtualization technologies, cloud computing and security, network and computer security as well as network and computer forensics is preferred. Applicants who specialize in and have a proven track record of research in cloud computing and security are especially encouraged to apply. A PhD in computer science and a strong record of scholarship demonstrated through publications in peer-reviewed proceedings and/or journals, or demonstrated potential for such, are required at the time of appointment. Candidates in the final year of their PhD, who will defend their thesis before the appointment date, are also encouraged to apply. An ideal candidate would have some teaching experience and a passion for educating. Candidates at the Associate rank must in addition have a track record of successful grant funding from state, federal or private sources. John Jay enjoys a cosmopolitan student body, reflective of the great cultural variety found in our home city of New York and welcomes all qualified applicants. **COMPENSATION** Salary commensurate with academic accomplishments and experience within the range of $59,256 - $114,091. CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** If you are viewing the job posting on any website other than CUNYFirst, please follow the instructions below: -Go to www.cuny.edu/employment -Click \"Search job postings. -Click the link for Faculty and browse to job Opening ID number 32823 -Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions Once registered or logged in, candidates should submit the following: an application letter with a statement of teaching and research interests and on your contribution to the goals of our college, C.V., proof of degree or progress toward the degree, and two letters of recommendations from academic or professional sources. All items to be uploaded must be combined in a single document preferably in PDF format. **CLOSING DATE** Posting closes on November 15, 2026. **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32823 Location: John Jay College Job Type: Full-Time"
    }
  },
